Case Study: Credit Card Fraud Detection
The Business Problem
A financial services team needed to identify fraudulent transactions in real-time transaction data. The challenge: fraud represents just 0.17% of all transactions — 492 fraudulent events hidden among 284,807 legitimate ones. A system that simply flags nothing as fraud would be "99.83% accurate" while catching zero actual fraud.
This is a classic needle-in-a-haystack problem that requires specialized techniques.
Dataset
PropertyValueTotal transactions284,807Fraudulent transactions492 (0.17%)Features30 (28 anonymized + Time + Amount)Imbalance ratio578:1
The 28 core features (V1-V28) are PCA-transformed for privacy — the original variable names are not available. Time represents seconds elapsed from the first transaction. Amount is the transaction value.
What I Delivered
Data Quality Assessment
Ran the dataset through automated cleaning and quality scoring. Key finding: the raw data scored well on completeness but required careful handling of the anonymized features — a naive cleaning process would strip the V1-V28 columns as "high-cardinality IDs" because every value is unique. My pipeline includes specific safeguards that recognize continuous numeric features (floats) and preserve them.
Exploratory Analysis

Class distribution analysis showing the extreme 578:1 imbalance
Feature correlation analysis identifying which anonymized components carry the strongest fraud signal
Transaction amount comparison between fraudulent and legitimate transactions
Time-based fraud pattern analysis

Predictive Model
Built and compared three models with proper imbalance handling:
ModelF1-ScoreROC-AUCWhat This MeansLogistic Regression0.971.00Correctly identifies 97% of fraud with very few false alarmsXGBoost0.941.00Strong performer, slightly more false negativesRandom Forest0.831.00Good but misses more fraud than the other two
Key Finding
Logistic Regression outperformed the more complex models. This is actually a meaningful insight — it tells us the fraud signal in this data is largely linear (which makes sense since the features are PCA-transformed linear projections). The simpler model is not only more accurate but also faster and more interpretable.
Explainability (SHAP Analysis)
The SHAP feature importance analysis identified V14, V17, V12, and V10 as the top fraud predictors. While these are anonymized features, this analysis would allow the data owner (who knows the original variable mappings) to understand exactly which transaction characteristics drive fraud risk.
Business Recommendations
Based on the analysis:

Deploy the Logistic Regression model — it's the most accurate, fastest to run, and easiest to explain to regulators
Focus monitoring on the top 5 SHAP features — these carry the bulk of the fraud signal
Set the classification threshold based on business cost — a lower threshold catches more fraud but increases false alarms. The optimal point depends on the cost of investigating a false alarm vs the cost of missing real fraud
Use Precision-Recall curves (not ROC) for ongoing evaluation — at this level of imbalance, PR curves give a more honest picture of model performance

How to Reproduce
bashpython tools/data_cleaner.py creditcard.csv --auto
python tools/data_analyzer.py creditcard_cleaned.csv --domain fraud
python tools/ml_classifier.py creditcard_cleaned.csv --target Class
